                <pb facs="0898" n="2600" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v09p2/pg2600"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">780.022/4–1554: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 1558<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Saudi Arabia
                        (Wadsworth) to the Department of State</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d1558-f1"
                    /></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Jidda</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1954-04-15T16:00:00+03:00">April 15, 1954—4 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>432. Since receipt Deptel 282, April 7,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d1558-f2"/>
                    following items re eastern frontier dispute and related matters have come my
                    attention:</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item><p>AramcoVice Presidents Duce and Ohliger were received by King Saud in
                            private audience April 7 and 8 in Riyadh and again April 10 at Dhahran
                            on eve His Majesty’s departure on official visits Bahrain and Pakistan.
                            Aramco Jidda representative Owen tells me:</p>
                        <p>At April 7 meeting King did most of talking. He liked film “The Arab
                            Island” Duce had shown him but suggested it be brought up to date by
                            including something regarding his own succession. Then with view
                            bringing Duce up to date SAG thinking, King made <hi rendition="#italic"
                                >inter alia</hi> following points:</p>
                        <gap/>
                        <p>At April 8 meeting Ohliger handed King letter (mytel 423, April 6<anchor
                                n="3" xml:id="d1558-f3"/>) formally asserting Aramco position re no
                            suspended concessionary rights in eastern disputed areas. . . . Further
                            discussion was reserved for April 10 meeting subject of which I have not
                            yet learned.</p>
                        <p>Duce and Ohliger were also told King had fixed date (April 23) for
                            resumption important discussion begun January 1952 regarding price
                            Aramco receives from parent companies for crude oil off-take and related
                            questions.</p></item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>British Ambassador Pelham has told me of King’s proposal March 21 through
                        British political agent Pelley at Kuwait for settlement eastern frontier
                        claims by reverting to direct negotiation. In reply Foreign Office’s request
                        for comment, Pelham urged strongly that while informal negotiations direct
                        bilateral settlement <pb facs="0899" n="2601" xml:id="pg2601"/> might prove
                        helpful, agreement to arbitrate dispute should be fully maintained pending
                        their upshot; and he seemed pleased Burrows had been named British
                        negotiator and instructed proceed on that basis. He will consult with
                        Burrows on Bahrain next week. He does not know whether King has appointed
                        Saudi negotiator.</item>
                </list>
                <p>Pelham also again insisted he had given Saudis no encouragement whatsoever in
                    reply Shaikh Yusuf’s last summer’s <hi rendition="#italic">démarches</hi> re
                    “arranging” that Aramco renounce concessionary rights in disputed areas; it was
                    only thereafter that Saudi Ambassador Wahbe apparently on orders sent him by
                    Shaikh Yusuf had made suggestion to Foreign Office which had led it to inject
                    oil issue into February 14 proposals.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold"><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Wadsworth</hi></hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
